NAME
       sudoers - the rules that decide who may run what with sudo

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/sudoers
       /etc/sudoers.d/*

DESCRIPTION
       sudo reads its rules from /etc/sudoers, which on RHEL 9 ends with the
       line "#includedir /etc/sudoers.d" (the # is part of the keyword here,
       not a comment). Every file in that directory is read too, in name
       order, except names that contain a dot or end in ~. Keep your own
       rules in separate files there; the main file stays as the package
       installed it.

       Always edit with visudo (visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/NAME for a file of
       its own): it checks the syntax before saving. A broken file can stop
       sudo from working for everyone. Files should have mode 0440 and be
       owned by root.

   Rules
       A user rule has this form:

           WHO  WHERE=(AS-WHOM)  [TAG:]  COMMANDS

       WHO      a user name, %group for every member of a group, or an
                alias.
       WHERE    the host names this rule applies to; ALL almost always.
       AS-WHOM  the users (and :groups) the commands may run as; (ALL) means
                anyone, including root. Left out, only root.
       TAG      NOPASSWD: lets the commands run without asking for the
                user's password; PASSWD: is the default.
       COMMANDS a comma-separated list of full program paths, with
                arguments if they must match exactly, or ALL. A directory
                path ending in / allows every program in it.

       When several rules match, the LAST one wins, so put exceptions after
       general rules. Lines starting with # are comments (except #include
       and #includedir).

   Aliases
       Names in capitals stand for lists:

           User_Alias  ADMINS = harry, natasha
           Cmnd_Alias  NETWORK = /usr/bin/nmcli, /usr/sbin/ip
           Host_Alias  WEB = server1, server2

   Defaults
       Lines starting with Defaults change sudo's behaviour, for example
       Defaults timestamp_timeout=10 (minutes a password is remembered) or
       Defaults:harry !requiretty.

   The RHEL 9 defaults
       root    ALL=(ALL)       ALL
       %wheel  ALL=(ALL)       ALL

       So members of the group wheel may run anything as anyone, after typing
       their own password.

EXAMPLES
       Let the members of the group admins run every command as root:

           %admins ALL=(ALL) ALL

       The same without a password:

           %admins ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL

       Let the user natasha manage users and nothing else:

           natasha ALL=(root) /usr/sbin/useradd, /usr/sbin/usermod, /usr/bin/passwd

       Let the group webteam restart the web server:

           %webteam ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd

       Create such a file and check it:

           echo '%admins ALL=(ALL) ALL' > /etc/sudoers.d/admins
           chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/admins
           visudo -c

NOTES
       Allowing an editor, a shell or a program that can start a shell (vim,
       less, bash, find with -exec) is the same as allowing everything.

       Group membership changes need a new login before sudo sees them.

FILES
       /etc/sudoers, /etc/sudoers.d/
              The rules.

SEE ALSO
